@Tutorial(time: 45) {
    @Intro(title: "Turn Your Room Into an Aquarium") {
        Point the iPad camera at your actual room and float the aquarium
        in the space around you.

        > Important: This chapter needs a real device (iPad running
        > iPadOS 18 or later).

        @Image(source: "03-intro", alt: "An AR aquarium placed in a real room")
    }

    @Section(title: "Switching to the Camera") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            So far RealityView has shown the scene through a virtual camera.
            Change the camera mode to `.spatialTracking` so that your own
            room becomes the background.

            @Image(source: "03-section1", alt: "A room seen through camera passthrough, with the world origin marked")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Go back to Xcode and open the RoomAquarium project settings.
                To use the camera you have to state why and get the user's
                permission. In **Build Settings**, find the Info.plist Values
                list, look for `Privacy - Camera Usage Description`, and write
                "We use the camera to turn your room into an aquarium."

                If the entry isn't there, add it yourself with the + button
                and then fill it in.

                @Image(source: "03-s1-step1", alt: "The camera permission string in Build Settings")
            }

            @Step {
                Setting content.camera to .spatialTracking makes the rear camera
                feed the background and starts the device tracking the space.

                On top of that, trackingSession.run starts a SpatialTrackingSession
                and asks for .occlusion and .shadow. Now real objects hide the
                seahorse and a shadow falls on the floor. Both features need the
                3D shape of the space, so they only work on devices with LiDAR.

                A session isn't something you run once and forget — it keeps
                running, and tracking stops when the object goes away. So it lives
                in @State, which holds the session for as long as the view is on screen.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Passthrough and camera controls **cannot be used together.** The
                moment the rear camera feed becomes the background, the camera
                controls you dragged to orbit the view stop working.

                So split them with `#if targetEnvironment(simulator)`. The
                simulator gets the virtual camera and orbit controls; the device
                gets passthrough. No more commenting and uncommenting every time
                you change the run destination.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Connect the iPad to your Mac and run against the device.
                Allow camera access and your room appears as the background,
                with the seahorse floating right there. Pick the device up
                and walk around it.

                @Image(source: "03-s1-result", alt: "The aquarium floating in a room, running on a device")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Interacting With the Seahorse") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Make the seahorse spin once and play a sound when you tap it.

            @Image(source: "03-section2", alt: "The seahorse reacting to a tap")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In **Timelines** in the Editor Panel at the bottom, create a
                new timeline and name it `TapSeahorse`.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step1", alt: "An empty timeline named TapSeahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Drag **Emphasize** out of the action list and drop it in.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step2", alt: "An Emphasize action placed on a timeline track")
            }

            @Step {
                Set Target to `Seahorse` and Motion Type to `Spin`.
                Set Duration to 1.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step3", alt: "Target, Motion Type, and Duration settings for the Emphasize action")
            }

            @Step {
                On the track just below Emphasize, place a **Play Audio** action, set
                Target to `Seahorse`, and pick the `WhaleCry.usdz` audio you registered
                in Chapter 2. Set Duration to 1.

                They have to be on separate tracks to play at the **same time** —
                put them on one track and they play in sequence, so watch out.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step4", alt: "Emphasize and Play Audio side by side on two tracks")
            }

            @Step {
                Select `Seahorse` and add an On Tap trigger to its **Behaviors**
                component. Set the timeline to run to TapSeahorse, then save (Cmd+S).

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step5", alt: "An On Tap trigger wired to TapSeahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Back to Xcode. Take the tap gesture and call applyTapForBehaviors()
                on the tapped entity — the seahorse. That one call is what fires the
                On Tap Behavior you set up on it.

                Later you can change the Behavior's On Tap timeline in Reality
                Composer Pro and the seahorse behaves differently without opening
                Xcode at all.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Run it and tap the seahorse. If it spins once while a whale
                call plays, it worked. This works in the simulator too.

                If nothing happens, suspect the **Collision size from Chapter 1**.
                That's the most common cause. If you entered something like `0.12`
                as if it were meters, the box is 200 times smaller than the seahorse
                and your finger can never reach it. Select `Seahorse` in RCP and
                check with your own eyes that the green wireframe wraps its body.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-result", alt: "The seahorse spinning in response to a tap")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "The Timeline Talks Back to Your Code") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Everything so far went one way: code → RCP. Now build the
            **other direction**.

            A timeline can hold a **Notification** action. When playback reaches
            that point, it sends a notification to the app. You decide in RCP,
            in time, when "the whale call starts" and when "it's over" — and the
            code only has to react at those moments.

            This is powerful because **the timing leaves your code**. To delay
            the performance by half a second you drag the action along the
            timeline. Nothing like `Task.sleep(0.5)` has to be buried in code.

            Here are the two notifications you'll place in the next step.

            - `SeahorseStartled` — at time `0`. The moment of the tap.
            - `SeahorseCalmed` — at time `1.9`. Where the whale call ends.

            Set **Target to `Seahorse`** for both.

            @Image(source: "03-section3", alt: "Notification actions on the timeline and the receiving side in code")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the `TapSeahorse` timeline in RCP and place two
                **Notification** actions on a new track. Fill in the names and
                times from the list above, then save.

                @Image(source: "03-s3-step1", alt: "Two Notification actions placed on the timeline")
            }

            @Step {
                Receive the notifications in code. Everything RCP sends arrives
                under a single name, `"RealityKit.NotificationTrigger"`, and
                **which action it was is told apart by the `Identifier` in
                `userInfo`**.

                Take that and show a badge on screen. The reaction is modest for
                now, but in Chapter 4 this same notification changes the seahorse's
                **state** and makes it actually move differently.

                > Note: `userInfo` also carries `SourceEntity`. But that is
                > **not the Target you set on the Notification action.** There's a
                > trap there, so Chapter 4 covers it separately. You don't use it here.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-04.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Run it and tap the seahorse: the badge appears, then disappears
                1.9 seconds later when the whale call ends.

                Look again at what you just built — **RCP decided "when," and the
                code decided "what."** The robot in Apple's official sample turns
                its watering can on and off with exactly this structure. This is as
                far as one seahorse goes. Chapter 4 turns it into many.

                @Image(source: "03-s3-result", alt: "The badge shown on screen in response to a tap")
            }
        }
    }
}
